{"id":"6c1b5b13-be1b-435e-8319-9ba0d3104b5a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785025530,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"import heapq\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\\n    heap = []\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while heap:\\n        neg_depth, node_id = heapq.heappop(heap)\\n        visit_order.append(node_id)\\n        if node_id not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_map[node_id]\\n        is_leaf = len(children[node_id]) == 0\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold and is_leaf:\\n            retained.remove(node_id)\\n            parent_id = node['parent']\\n            if parent_id is not None:\\n                if node_id in children[parent_id]:\\n                    children[parent_id].remove(node_id)\\n                if parent_id in retained:\\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, (-node_map[parent_id]['depth'], parent_id))\\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_node_ids, \\\"visit_order\\\": visit_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` that processes the given payload. The payload contains a list of nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also utility_threshold. The task: \"Implement parse_tree_reduction_queue through solve(case) for this bounded objective: Maintain and update the syntax-guided reduction work queue across accepted and rejected tree rewrites without revisiting invalidated nodes.\"\n\nFrom the description: We have a tree with nodes. We need to perform reduction: remove removable leaf nodes whose utility <= threshold. After removal, enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. We need to return retained_node_ids (IDs of nodes that are kept after all removals) and visit_order (the order in which nodes were visited/processed). The queue is initialized in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Skip nodes already removed. Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold. After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\n\nInterpretation: We have a tree. Initially, we need to create a queue of nodes (by id? or references?) sorted by descending depth, and for same depth, stable node-ID order (presumably alphabetical or lexical order of IDs). Then process queue: dequeue, if that node is already removed, skip. Else if it is a leaf (no children? But note that after removals, children might be gone, so leaf condition is dynamic: a node that currently has no children that are retained? Or originally no children? The description says \"leaf\" - likely a node that has no children in the current tree (i.e., after removals, its children have been removed). But the initial queue might include non-leaf nodes? The queue initialization in descending depth, so all nodes are included. Then we process: for each node dequeued, if it is removable and its utility <= threshold and it is a leaf (no children that are retained), then we remove it. After removal, we enqueue its parent (if parent exists and parent is retained) for reconsideration. So we need to track the tree structure and update children sets as removals happen.\n\nOutput: retained_node_ids: list of IDs of nodes that remain (not removed). visit_order: list of node IDs in the order they were dequeued (processed). Even if a node is skipped because already removed, it still might be visited? The spec says \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" The visit order likely includes every time we dequeue a node (even if skipped?) But careful: \"Skip nodes already removed.\" That might mean we skip processing them, but do we still count them in visit order? Probably not, because they are skipped. The description: \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order. Skip nodes already removed. Remove only a removable leaf ... After removal enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration.\" The \"complete visit order\" likely is the order in which nodes are dequeued and processed (i.e., we attempt to process them). Skipped nodes are still dequeued, so they are visited. However, there's nuance: \"without revisiting invalidated nodes\" – once a node is removed, we shouldn't revisit it. But we might skip it if it's already removed. So the visit order includes all nodes that are dequeued (including those that are skipped because already removed). But we need to ensure we don't enqueue a node multiple times? The algorithm might cause re-enqueuing of parent after removal. Should we avoid duplicate entries in queue? We can use a set to track if a node is already in queue, to avoid duplicates. But the order: when a parent is re-enqueued, its depth is less than its children, so it will be placed after all nodes at that depth? The queue is sorted initially, but we need to maintain order after insertions. The problem likely expects a specific deterministic order. The description says \"Initialize a queue in descending depth and stable node-ID order.\" That suggests the initial queue is sorted. After that, when we enqueue a parent for reconsideration, we insert it at a position that maintains the ordering (desc depth, then stable node-ID). Typically, we could use a priority queue (heap) with key = (-depth, node_id). But node_ids are strings; stable order means lexical order. So we can use a heap with tuple (-depth, node_id). However, heap in Python is min-heap, so we can use (depth, node_id) but we want descending depth, so we can use (-depth, node_id) to have highest depth first. For stable tie-breaking, node_id lexical order (ascending). That matches typical.\n\nBut careful: The queue may contain duplicate entries if a node is enqueued multiple times. We need to skip duplicates (skip if already removed). We should also avoid adding a node if it has already been removed? But after removal, its parent is enqueued; we should add parent only if it's retained (not removed). Also, we need to know if a node is currently still in the tree (retained). We can maintain a set of retained node IDs. Initially all are retained. When we remove a node, we remove it from retained set. Also, we need to know children of each node to check if a node is leaf. We can maintain a dict of children list for each node. Initially build from parent references. When a node is removed, we should remove it from its parent's children list. Then the parent becomes a leaf if its children list becomes empty. That condition is used when processing the parent when it is dequeued later.\n\nNow, algorithm:\n\n- Parse case.nodes into list of dicts.\n- Build a dict node_by_id = {node['id']: node}\n- Build children dict: children = {id: [] for all ids}. For each node, if parent is not None, children[parent].append(node['id']).\n- Initialize retained = set(all ids).\n- Initialize queue as a heap (list) with entries (-depth, node_id) for all nodes. (depth from node['depth'])\n- Initialize visit_order = [].\n- Process while queue:\n   - Pop smallest from heap (since min-heap, we get (-depth, node_id) with deepest nodes first; for same depth, lower node_id first because we used stable order). Actually heap compares tuples, so (-depth, node_id) with larger depth (more negative) comes first. For equal depth, node_id lexical order. This matches descending depth, then stable node-ID order? \"Stable node-ID order\" likely means alphabetical order. So descending depth, then alphabetical order. That is what we get.\n   - node_id = popped[1]\n   - visit_order.append(node_id)\n   - If node_id not in retained: skip (already removed). Continue.\n   - node = node_by_id[node_id]\n   - Check if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= utility_threshold and is_leaf(node_id): where is_leaf means len(children[node_id]) == 0 (no children currently retained? Actually children list only contains IDs that are still retained? We should update children list when we remove. So is_leaf if children[node_id] is empty.\n   - If condition true: remove node_id from retained. Then for each child in children[node_id]? No, children already empty because leaf. But we also need to remove node from its parent's children list. So parent_id = node['parent']; if parent_id: remove node_id from children[parent_id] list. Also, after removal, if parent_id is retained, enqueue parent for reconsideration: push (-depth[parent], parent_id) onto heap. But we might want to avoid duplicates by checking if parent_id is already in heap? Could cause many duplicates. Better to allow duplicates and skip when popped (already removed check). But we must ensure that we don't infinitely loop? Since parent depth is less, it will be popped later. Could be enqueued multiple times; that's fine, we skip duplicates. However, we should not enqueue a parent that is already removed (but it's retained, so OK). Also, after removal, the parent may become leaf later; but we only check leaf condition when it is popped. That's fine.\n   - Else: node not removed, do nothing (no further enqueue).\n\nEdge cases: root node is not removable (from sample, removable=false). So root will never be removed. After all removals, root and others remain.\n\nWe need to return retained_node_ids list, sorted? Not specified. The example output structure says array of strings, but order? Probably the order of retained IDs as they remain in the tree? Not specified; likely any order is fine as long as correct set. But we can sort them for determinism? The typical expectation is perhaps the order in which nodes were last visited? Or no specific order. Since the output contract only requires array of strings, order might not matter, but to be safe, we can output in ascending order of IDs? Let's check the fixture examples? Not provided expected output. We'll assume set as list in some consistent order, e.g., sorted by ID or by depth? I'll output sorted by ID for simplicity.\n\nvisit_order should be order of dequeuing, as we appended.\n\nNow implement solve(case) function.\n\nWe need to read payload fields: case['nodes'], case['utility_threshold'].\n\nAlso note there is '_case_nonce' but ignore.\n\nImplementation details: Use heapq.import heapq.\n\nWe need to retain stable node-ID order for same depth: stable means consistent with original insertion order? But we use lexical, which is stable.\n\nPotential pitfalls: When we enqueue parent after removal, we compute depth from node_by_id[parent_id]['depth']. That's fine.\n\nNow, we need to return a JSON object with keys \"retained_node_ids\" and \"visit_order\". The values must be lists of strings.\n\nNow, about the submission envelope: The entire response must be one valid JSON object with single key \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a JSON string. So we need to encode the Python source into a JSON string, escaping newlines, quotes, backslashes. We'll produce a Python source string and then JSON-encode it.\n\nWe need to ensure no imports beyond standard library. We'll use heapq.\n\nWrite the solve function.\n\nLet's draft the Python code:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    \n    # Build data structures\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    \n    retained = set(n['id'] for n in nodes)\n    \n    # Initialize heap: ( -depth, node_id ) for descending depth, then ascending id\n    heap = []\n    for n in nodes:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    \n    visit_order = []\n    \n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, node_id = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        visit_order.append(node_id)\n        \n        if node_id not in retained:\n            continue\n        \n        node = node_map[node_id]\n        # leaf check: no children in current retained tree\n        # children list may contain IDs that are still retained; we should check if any children are retained?\n        # Actually children list only contains IDs that were originally children; we only remove from children list when child is removed.\n        # So if children[node_id] is empty, it's a leaf.\n        is_leaf = len(children[node_id]) == 0\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold and is_leaf:\n            # Remove this node\n            retained.remove(node_id)\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n            if parent_id is not None:\n                # Remove from parent's children list\n                if node_id in children[parent_id]:\n                    children[parent_id].remove(node_id)\n                # Enqueue parent for reconsideration if parent is retained\n                if parent_id in retained:\n                    heapq.heappush(heap, (-node_map[parent_id]['depth'], parent_id))\n    \n    # Compute results\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained))  # sorted for deterministic output\n    # visit_order as is (strings)\n    return {\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids,\n        \"visit_order\": visit_order\n    }\n```\n\nBut careful: The children list modification: if parent is removed later, we might still have its children list non-empty? But only removed nodes are removed from children of parent. This is fine.\n\nAlso, when we check is_leaf, we rely on children list being up-to-date. But note that a node may have children that were never removed; then it's not leaf. If all its children